Does interface solve diamond problem?
Interfaces help address the diamond problem in multiple inheritance, but indirectly:
The diamond problem arises when a class inherits from two parent classes that define methods with the same name and signature, leading to ambiguity.
In Java:
Classes do not support multiple inheritance, which avoids this issue.
Interfaces support multiple inheritance, but there’s no ambiguity because a class implementing multiple interfaces must explicitly define the methods it inherits.
If a default method in two interfaces clashes, the implementing class must override it to resolve the conflict explicitly.
